          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-different" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why Powder Bakes Differently</a>
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#protein-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. How Each Protein Type Bakes</a>
          <a href="#substitution" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Substitution Ratios</a>
          <a href="#moisture-heat" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Moisture, Heat and Timing</a>
          <a href="#heat-nutrition" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Does Baking Destroy Protein?</a>
          <a href="#by-recipe" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. What to Bake With Which</a>
          <a href="#sweeteners" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Flavored vs Unflavored</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most protein baking fails for one reason: people pick the leanest, purest powder they own, usually a whey isolate, and swap it cup for cup with flour. Isolate sets firm the moment it gets hot, so the result is dry, springy and faintly rubbery. The best protein powder for baking is usually the opposite of the best one for a post-workout shake: a whey and casein blend, a casein, or a plant powder that holds onto water. Here are six that suit the oven, and the technique that matters just as much as the tub.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-different" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Protein Powder Behaves Differently in the Oven
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flour does three jobs in a cake or muffin. Its starch soaks up water and swells as it heats, its gluten proteins form a stretchy network that traps gas, and together they set into a soft crumb. Protein powder can only copy part of that. It has no starch to swell and no gluten to stretch, so when it replaces flour the batter loses the structure that makes baked goods tender rather than tough.
          </p>

          <p>
            What protein powder does instead is coagulate. Whey proteins unfold when heated and bond to each other, which is the same thing that happens when an egg white sets. A little of that firms a batter usefully. A lot of it, especially from a fast-setting whey isolate with almost no fat or lactose, gives a crumb that springs back like a sponge and dries out within hours. That is the rubbery, squeaky texture people complain about in homemade protein muffins.
          </p>

          <p>
            Powders also drink liquid. Casein and plant proteins in particular absorb far more water than the flour they replace, so a recipe that looked right before the oven can bake into something dense and dry. Sweetened powders add another variable, because many contain sucralose, stevia or sugar alcohols that change browning and sweetness. Baking with protein powder works well once you treat it as its own ingredient with its own rules rather than as a straight flour substitute.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Pick a powder that holds water, and never swap it for all the flour.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Blends with casein and plant proteins stay softer than isolate. Replace roughly a quarter to a third of the flour, add moisture, lower the oven a little and pull the bake early.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked for Baking Performance
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run a test kitchen for this guide. The picks come from label analysis, protein type, ingredient lists and what large numbers of buyers report about baking with each product, which is the method on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Where we describe how a powder behaves in batter, that is a summary of public buyer feedback and the food science of each protein type, not our own result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein type mattered most. Whey and casein blends and straight caseins scored highest because casein holds water and sets more softly than whey isolate. Whey concentrate came next, since its small amount of fat and lactose makes for a slightly more tender crumb than isolate. Plant powders earned a place for absorbing moisture well and for dairy-free bakers, with the caveat that their earthy flavor suits chocolate, banana and spice better than plain vanilla.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also looked at protein per scoop, so that the finished bake still delivers a meaningful dose, and at the sweetener system, because a heavily sweetened powder forces you to cut sugar elsewhere. We favored products sold widely on Amazon and in stores with long track records. We did not reward added creatine, fat burners or other extras, which do nothing for a muffin and are better taken, if at all, separately.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Protein Powders for Baking, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            The six picks below are ordered by how forgiving they are in typical home recipes such as muffins, pancakes, brownies and quick breads. Protein figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor; check the tub you buy. Where we mention texture or taste, we are passing on what buyers widely report, not a result from our own kitchen.
          </p>

