        <div id="protein-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Whey, Casein, Blends and Plant: How Each One Bakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whey isolate is the hardest powder to bake with. It is roughly 90 percent protein with almost no fat or lactose, mixes thin and sets fast. It works in small amounts, a scoop in a batch of pancakes or a few tablespoons in cookies, but it turns cakes and muffins dry if it carries much of the structure. If isolate is the only tub you own, keep it to a quarter of the flour and lean on yogurt or mashed fruit for moisture. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide covers isolates for shakes, where they shine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Casein is the friendliest dairy option. It forms a thicker, gel-like mix with liquid and holds onto water during baking, so brownies, blondies, mug cakes and cookies stay fudgy. Too much makes things dense, which is a good trade in a brownie and a poor one in a sponge cake. Our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> rankings go deeper on casein in general.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey and casein blends sit in the middle and are the most forgiving all-rounders. The casein softens the whey's setting, and many blends are formulated to taste like a milkshake, so they double as a sweetener and flavoring in batter. Plant powders such as pea or pea and rice blends absorb the most liquid of all and give a moist, slightly dense crumb with an earthy note. They suit dairy-free bakers and are strongest in banana bread, chocolate muffins and spiced bakes.
          </p>

