        <div id="substitution" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Substitution Ratios That Actually Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most useful rule in protein baking is also the simplest: replace part of the flour, not all of it. Swapping roughly a quarter to a third of the flour by volume keeps enough starch and gluten for structure while adding a real amount of protein. Above about half, most recipes stop behaving like the original, and you are better off following a recipe written from scratch for protein powder rather than adapting a standard one.
          </p>

          <p>
            For every scoop of powder you add, expect to add liquid too. A standard scoop is around 30 g, roughly a third of a cup, and a common starting point is two to four tablespoons of extra liquid per scoop for whey, and more for casein and plant powder. The liquid does not have to be water: milk, Greek yogurt, pumpkin purée, applesauce or mashed banana add moisture and soften the crumb at the same time. The batter should look a little looser than the original recipe, because the powder keeps absorbing as it sits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flavored powders are also a sweetener. A vanilla or chocolate scoop sweetened with sucralose or stevia can be several times sweeter than the sugar it sits beside, so reduce the sugar in the recipe by a quarter to a half the first time and taste the batter. Do not remove all of it in yeast breads or cookies where sugar also helps browning and spread. Keep notes, because the right ratio depends on the specific powder, and two tubs from different brands can behave quite differently.
          </p>

